  "title": "Basic Concepts of Electricity",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "695986688",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Three photographs are shown in this figure. The first \n            shows lightning against a dark evening sky. The second shows a child at the open base of a green plastic playground tube slide. \n            The child’s hair is sticking up and the child’s shadow on the base of the slide shows the child’s hair sticking up and out in \n            all directions. The final picture shows a 9 volt battery from which red and blue coated wire that is twisted together extend \n            from the battery terminals to the lower region of a yellow platform or board. Above this region are six resistors in a horizontal \n            row, evenly spaced horizontally across the span of the board. Green, blue, and white wires are also visible on the board. \n            6 orange L E D light bulbs extend from the upper edge of the platform in a horizontal line parallel to the pegs.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Electricity-related phenomena include lightning, accumulation of static electricity, and current produced by a battery. (Credits: Modification of work by Thomas Bresson [left]; Chris Darling [middle]; Windell Oskay [right].)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "uyd8op5j1frchtz",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "isprxxjrv63qfwn",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/be/be2dd2286e4130d60162f607673cb099/CNX_Chem_17_01_Electric.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "qos4r2o7r1u9e8q",
                "text": "Introduction to Electricity"
              }
            ],
            "id": "qm6aakzshgngqky",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electricity refers to a number of phenomena associated with the presence and flow of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2056165400",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3650359087",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "electric charge"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The physical property of matter that causes it to experience a force when in an electromagnetic field. Charge can be positive or negative; like charges repel and unlike charges attract each other."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2056165400",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3650359087",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". It includes such diverse phenomena as lightning, static electricity, the current generated by a battery as it discharges, and many other influences on our daily lives."
              }
            ],
            "id": "9reao1nv7kkfp62",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Electric current"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A flow of charged particles that move through a material or space; the net rate of flow of electric charge through a surface."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3460949733",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4184981996",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the flow or movement of charged particles, such as electrons or ions. The elementary unit of charge is the charge of a proton, which is equal in magnitude to the charge of an electron. The SI unit of charge is the coulomb (C), and the charge of a proton is 1.602 × 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−19"
              },
              {
                "text": " C. The presence of an electric charge generates an electric field. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "xt65qqheyt7npxv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electric current is the rate of flow of charge. The SI unit for electrical current is the SI base unit called the ampere (A), which is a flow rate of 1 coulomb of charge per second (1 A = 1 C/s). An electric current flows in a path called an electric circuit. In most chemical systems, it is necessary to maintain a closed path for current to flow. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "2of0gx6fxtv744x",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The flow of charge is generated by an electrical potential difference, or potential, between two points in the circuit. "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Electrical potential"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The amount of energy needed to move a unit charge from a reference point to a specific point in an electric field."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1529446027",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2520055905",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the ability of the electric field to do work on the charge. The SI unit of electrical potential is the volt, V (V = J/C). When 1 coulomb of charge moves through a potential difference of 1 volt, it gains or loses 1 joule (J) of energy. The table below summarizes some of this information about electricity."
              }
            ],
            "id": "wjlan5obyfzdxrs",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
